  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Return to your original group and work through this next part of your assigned problem. Then be ready to share.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">When Raoul runs on the treadmill at the gym, the number of calories, \(c\), he burns varies directly with the number of minutes, \(m\), he uses the treadmill. He burned 315 calories when he used the treadmill for 18 minutes. </para>
          <para id="para-00003">How many calories would he burn if he ran on the treadmill for 25 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\( c=437.5 \), so Raoul would burn 437.5 calories if he used the treadmill for 25 minutes.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">The number of calories, \(c\), burned varies directly with the amount of time, \(t\), spent exercising. Arnold burned 312 calories in 65 minutes exercising. </para>
          <para id="para-00007">How many calories would he burn if he exercises for 90 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\( c=432 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">The distance a moving body travels, \(d\), varies directly with time, \(t\), it moves. A train travels 100 miles in 2 hours.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">How many miles would it travel in 5 hours?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">250 miles</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014">For example 1, how could you find out how many minutes Raoul would need to use the treadmill to burn a certain number of calories?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Substitute the number of calories you want to burn into the equation for \( c \) and then solve for \( m \).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Video: Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems</title>
      <para id="para-00016">Watch the following video to learn more about how to use direct variation to solve an application problem.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/262332920b652e0ac822f2af48b9d1dc5c3c6baf"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00017">Sophia is making parts for a model ship. The number of parts, \( p \), she can make in a day varies directly with the number of hours, \( h \), she works. She can make 28 parts in 4 hours. How many parts would Sophia make in 9 hours?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
        <para id="para-00018">In applications using direct variation, generally we will know values of one pair of the variables and will be asked to find the equation that relates \( x \) and \( y \). Then we can use that equation to find values of \( y \) for other values of \( x \).</para>
        <para id="para-00019">Here’s how to solve applications of direct variation problems.</para>
        <note id="note-00002">
          <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables.</para>
        <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables.</para>
        <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation.</para>
        <para id="para-00023"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates \(x\) and \(y\) using the constant of variation.</para>
        <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable.</para>
        <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve.</para>
        </note>
        <para id="para-00026">Let’s look at an application problem:</para>
        <para id="para-00027">If the cost of a pizza varies directly with its diameter, and an 8-inch pizza costs $12, how much would a 6-inch pizza cost?</para>
        <para id="para-00028"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. \( y = kx \)</para>
        <para id="para-00029">\(y\): cost of pizza, \(x\): diameter</para>
        <para id="para-00030"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. \(12 = k(8)\)</para>
        <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. \(k= \frac32 \)</para>
        <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates x and y using the constant of variation. \( y= \frac{3}{2}x \)</para>
        <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. \( y= \frac{3}{2}(6) \)</para>
        <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. \( y = 9 \)</para>
        <para id="para-00035">What does this mean in the context of the problem?</para>
        <para id="para-00036">A pizza that has a diameter of 6 inches costs $9.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00003">
          <title> Try It: Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
            <para id="para-00037">The number of buckets of water, \(b\), filled by a spring varies directly with the number of minutes, \(m\), passed. The spring fills 3 buckets of water in 14 minutes.</para>
              <exercise id="exer-00005">
            <problem id="prob-00005">
              <para id="para-00038">How many buckets would be filled in 42 minutes?</para>
              <para id="para-00039">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00005">
              <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer: Here is how to model this application problem of direct variation.</para>
              <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. \( y = kx \) \( b=km \)</para>
              <para id="para-00042">\(b\): number of buckets, \(m\): number of minutes</para>
              <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. \( 3=k(14) \)</para>
              <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. \( k= 3/14 \)</para>
              <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates \(m\) and \(b\) using the constant of variation. \( b= \frac{3}{14}m \)</para>
              <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. \( b= \frac{3}{14}(42) \)</para>
              <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. \(b= 9\) buckets</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
